WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.328
Idea. If petrol gets dearer, drivers still need to fill up. If one brand of chocolate gets dearer, people switch to another. Same price rise, very different reactions.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:21.095
Elastic means quantity reacts a lot. So we draw elastic demand fairly flat. A small change in price gives a big change in quantity.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:31.141
Inelastic means quantity reacts only a little. So we draw inelastic demand steep. A big change in price gives a small change in quantity.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:41.991
Supply works the same way, but slopes upward. Elastic supply is flat: when demand rises, quantity supplied rises a lot and price only a little.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:54.929
Method. Step one. Decide: demand or supply, and elastic or inelastic. Demand slopes down, supply slopes up. Elastic is flat, inelastic is steep.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:07.644
Steps two to four. Draw the curve and label it. Pick two points. Draw dashed lines from each to the price axis, P one and P two, and to the quantity axis, Q one and Q two.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:16.621
Step five. Check the gaps. For elastic, the quantity gap must be clearly bigger than the price gap. For inelastic, the reverse.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:32.457
Worked example. Here is a real question from June 2024. Using the diagram, draw a price elastic demand curve. Label it, and show the impact on both axes from a change in price. Three marks.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:39.705
Demand slopes down, and elastic means fairly flat. Draw a gentle downward curve and label it D.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:48.777
Pick a higher point on the curve. Draw a dashed line across to the price axis, P one, and down to the quantity axis, Q one.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:02:05.993
Pick a slightly lower point. Mark P two just below P one, and Q two well to the right of Q one. A small price gap and a big quantity gap show elastic demand. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:13.785
Common mistake. A common mistake is swapping the slopes: drawing elastic steep and inelastic flat.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:21.793
Another is drawing the price gap and the quantity gap about the same size, or showing the change on only one axis.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:39.473
Your turn. Now you try. People need water every day, and a town has only one water company. Draw a price inelastic demand curve for water, label it, and show the effect of a price rise on both axes. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:53.949
Answer. Draw a steep demand curve and label it D. The price rises from P one to P two: a big gap. Quantity falls from Q one to Q two: a small gap. That shows inelastic demand.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:03:03.307
Remember: choose the slope, label the curve, and show both changes with dashed lines and clear gaps. Now try the practice questions.
